---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import {FAQSection, RelatedArticles} from '../../components/article';

const title = 'Complete Home Office Checklist: Everything You Need';
const description = 'The ultimate home office checklist for remote workers. From essential furniture to tech equipment, lighting, and ergonomics—build your perfect workspace.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2024-12-15');
const readingTime = 16;
const image = '/images/unsplash/photo-1518455027359-f3f8164ba6bd-1200w.webp';
const imageAlt = 'Well-organized home office setup with desk, chair, and equipment';

const headings = [
  {depth: 2, slug: 'furniture-checklist', text: 'Your Desk and Chair'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'tech-equipment', text: 'Tech Stuff'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'lighting-checklist', text: 'Getting the Light Right'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'organization-storage', text: 'Keeping Things Organized'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'comfort-ergonomics', text: 'Making It Comfortable'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'budget-tiers', text: 'What It Costs'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'printable-checklist', text: 'Short Version'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'faq', text: 'Common Questions'}
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: 'How much should I budget?',
    answer: 'Functional home office runs five hundred to eight hundred with budget options. Comfortable mid-range setup runs fifteen hundred to twenty five hundred. Premium setups with high-end ergonomic furniture and equipment hit three thousand to five thousand or beyond.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What matters most?',
    answer: 'Your chair. You spend eight plus hours a day sitting. Bad chair leads to back pain and long-term health issues. Invest in a quality ergonomic chair first even if you save money elsewhere.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Need a dedicated room?',
    answer: 'Nope. Many people work fine from a corner of a bedroom or living room. I\'ve even seen people convert large closets into office nooks. Just need a space that feels like work mode when you\'re there. And somewhere you can mentally walk away from at the end of the day.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Worth getting a standing desk?',
    answer: 'Standing desks let you alternate sitting and standing which has real health benefits. Not essential but if budget allows, sit-stand desk is worthwhile investment for the long haul.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Best monitor size?',
    answer: 'Twenty seven inches hits the sweet spot for most remote workers. Big enough for comfortable viewing and juggling windows without being overwhelming. Dual monitors or ultrawide at thirty four inches or more is great if you run multiple applications all day.'
  },
  {
    question: 'How important is lighting?',
    answer: 'Very. Bad lighting wrecks your eyes and gives you headaches. Pair natural light with a good desk lamp when possible. Watch out for screen glare and make sure your face looks decent on video calls.'
  }
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Ultimate Home Office Setup',
    excerpt: 'Complete guide to building your ideal remote workspace.',
    href: '/blog/ultimate-home-office-setup',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1518455027359-f3f8164ba6bd-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Modern home office setup'
  },
  {
    title: 'Best Standing Desks',
    excerpt: 'Top standing desks for health and productivity.',
    href: '/gear/best-standing-desks',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1593062096033-9a26b09da705-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Standing desk in home office'
  },
  {
    title: 'Best Ergonomic Chairs',
    excerpt: 'Find the perfect chair for long work sessions.',
    href: '/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1580480055273-228ff5388ef8-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Ergonomic office chair'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Setup"
    categorySlug="guides/setup"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Spent way too long overthinking my home office setup. Bought random stuff I thought I needed. Missed important
    things I actually did need. Six months in, finally figured out what matters.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is everything. Organized by category so you don't make the same mistakes I did.
  </p>

  <p>
    You don't need everything on day one. Start with essentials. Add stuff as your budget and needs become clearer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="furniture-checklist">Your Desk and Chair</h2>

  <p>
    The desk. Minimum forty eight inches wide for comfortable work. Sixty inches if you use multiple monitors. Standard
    height twenty eight to thirty inches but adjustable is ideal. At least twenty four inches deep so your monitor sits
    at proper distance. Cable management built in is a nice bonus.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chair. Most important purchase. Don't cheap out here.
  </p>

  <p>
    Adjustable height so your feet sit flat on the floor with thighs parallel to ground. Lumbar support that actually
    adjusts to prevent lower back pain. Armrests that move up and down so your elbows hit ninety degrees. Seat deep
    enough for two to four fingers of space between edge and back of your knees. Mesh back helps if you run hot.
  </p>

  <p>
    Other furniture to consider. Monitor stand or arm to raise screen to eye level. Footrest if your feet don't reach
    the floor comfortably. Filing cabinet or drawers for documents. Bookshelf for reference materials.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tech-equipment">Tech Stuff</h2>

  <p>
    Computing basics. <a href="/gear/best-laptops-remote-work">Computer</a> that matches your work requirements.
    External <a href="/gear/best-monitors-remote-work">monitor</a> at least twenty four to twenty
    seven inches. Larger or dual monitors if you're constantly multitasking. External keyboard if you use a laptop.
    Ergonomic or <a href="/gear/best-mechanical-keyboards">mechanical keyboard</a> for comfort. Mouse that won't destroy
    your wrist. Large mouse pad with wrist rest.
  </p>

  <p>
    Video call gear. <a href="/gear/best-webcams-video-calls">Webcam</a> at least 1080p. 4K if you do professional
    presentations. USB mic or decent headset. Built-in
    laptop mics sound terrible. Good <a href="/gear/best-noise-canceling-headphones">headphones</a> to hear calls
    clearly and block out distractions. Ring light or lamp
    aimed at your face so you don't look like a shadow on <a href="/tools/best-video-conferencing-tools">calls</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Connectivity. USB hub or dock gives you more ports to work with. Ethernet cable because wired beats wifi for
    reliability. Power strip with surge protection. UPS battery backup if power outages would wreck your workday.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lighting-checklist">Getting the Light Right</h2>

  <p>
    Natural light. Put your desk sideways to windows. Cuts the glare. Blinds or curtains let you control how much sun
    gets in. Bright window behind you ruins video calls.
  </p>

  <p>
    Artificial light. LED desk lamp with adjustable brightness. Overhead or floor lamp for ambient lighting that reduces
    harsh contrast. Task lighting for reading documents. Color temp around four to five thousand kelvin for work. Warmer
    in evenings. Light facing you at face level for video calls.
  </p>

  <h2 id="organization-storage">Keeping Things Organized</h2>

  <p>
    Desk organization. Pen holder to keep writing tools accessible. Document tray for incoming paperwork. Drawer
    organizers to prevent junk drawer chaos. Monitor shelf creates storage underneath. Desktop organizer for small items
    and sticky notes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cable management. Cable clips to keep things routed neatly. Cable sleeve to bundle multiple cables. Under-desk tray
    to hide power strips and excess cable. Velcro ties for adjustable bundling.
  </p>

  <p>
    Documents. Filing cabinet for important papers. Scanner to digitize documents and reduce paper clutter. Shredder for
    sensitive documents. Label maker to keep everything clearly marked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comfort-ergonomics">Making It Comfortable</h2>

  <p>
    Monitor positioning. Arms length from your eyes. Twenty to twenty six inches. Top of screen at or slightly below eye
    level. Slight backward tilt to reduce glare. If using multiple monitors, primary directly ahead with secondary at
    slight angle.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keyboard and mouse positioning. Arms bent at ninety degrees. Wrists straight. Wrist rest optional and better used
    during breaks than while typing. Mouse close to keyboard at same height.
  </p>

  <p>
    Comfort items. Footrest if your chair doesn't let feet sit flat. Cushion for extra padding during long sessions.
    Back support pillow if chair lumbar support falls short. Rubber mat in front of standing desks saves your feet and
    knees.
  </p>

  <p>
    Environment. Fan or space heater for when the temperature gets annoying. Plants make the air better and stress
    easier. White noise machine drowns out distractions. Air purifier helps especially in smaller spaces.
  </p>

  <h2 id="budget-tiers">What It Costs</h2>

  <p>
    Budget tier five hundred to eight hundred. Focus on essentials that directly impact work and health.
  </p>

  <p>
    Basic ergonomic chair runs a hundred fifty to two fifty. Simple desk costs a hundred to two hundred. External
    monitor hits a hundred fifty to two hundred. Keyboard and mouse together run fifty to a hundred. Desk lamp around
    thirty to fifty.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mid-range tier fifteen hundred to twenty five hundred. Better quality and more comfort features.
  </p>

  <p>
    Quality ergonomic chair four hundred to six hundred. Electric standing desk four hundred to six hundred. Twenty
    seven inch 4K monitor three hundred to four hundred. Quality webcam a hundred to a hundred fifty. Good headset a
    hundred to two hundred. Proper lighting setup a hundred to a hundred fifty.
  </p>

  <p>
    Premium tier three thousand to five thousand or more. The good stuff for serious remote workers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Premium <a href="/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs">chair</a> like Herman Miller or Steelcase runs a thousand to fifteen
    hundred. Motorized <a href="/gear/best-standing-desks">standing desk</a> with
    memory presets seven hundred to a thousand. Dual monitors or ultrawide six hundred to a thousand. Professional
    microphone setup two hundred to four hundred. 4K webcam two hundred to three hundred. Mechanical keyboard a hundred
    fifty to three hundred. High-end peripherals and accessories run three hundred to five hundred more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="printable-checklist">Short Version</h2>

  <p>
    Absolute essentials. Desk appropriate size for your work. Ergonomic chair. Computer. External monitor. External
    keyboard. Mouse. Desk lamp. Headset or headphones. Webcam. Solid internet connection.
  </p>

  <p>
    Worth adding soon. Monitor stand or arm. Cable management solution. Power strip with surge protection. USB hub or
    dock. Footrest if needed. Desk organizer. Good lighting for video calls.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nice to have eventually. Standing desk or desk converter. Noise canceling headphones. Second monitor. UPS battery
    backup. Document scanner. Plants. White noise machine.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Common Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Where to Begin</h2>

  <p>
    Grab the essentials first and skip the extras until later. Decent chair, desk, and your computing equipment. Then
    add items over time as you figure out what would actually improve your workday.
  </p>

  <p>
    Best home office is one you'll actually use comfortably for years. Invest in quality where it matters most. Chair,
    monitor, keyboard. Save on items where budget options work just as well. Your future self will appreciate taking
    time to set this up properly.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
